Jim Miller wrote:
...
> My broader point, though, is that it should matter little to us who
> "owns" the name "nethack".  In practice and culture the game is
> supported by many people, but under the present system ineffectually.
...

Unfortunately, this is probably the least accurate thing you've said.

It's _very_ important who owns the name NetHack; the folks on the 
DevTeam own it.  In my opinion, this is just obvious.

Any other package, regardless of whether it originates as a fork of 
NetHack itself, is _not_ NetHack; it's something else.  Common usage in 
the open source world would certainly allow a forked project to use its 
parent project's name as _part_ of its own name, but certainly not to 
claim the original name for its own.

NetHack is NetHack; anything else is not.  ;-)

-robin

(On the other hand, I'm all in favor of forks and variants; the more the 
merrier, I think.  I'm just responding here to what seems to be a claim 
that a forked project would have any right to call itself "NetHack".)


